I'm posting the second draft of a talk I'm writing in support of the
school choice measure that will be on the ballot in California this fall
in order to get constructive criticism of it from you.

Would you like better education for the students you care about?  Would you 
like to know what's wrong with the education they're getting now, and how 
it got that way?  If so, then please hear me out.

Better education is not only possible, it's happening almost everywhere 
besides public schools.  Inner-city students are flocking to private 
schools in droves.  SF State Black Studies Professor Laura Head says "I don't
know a black parent with a job whose children are in public school."
Inner-city private schools not only teach reading, writing, and arithmetic 
better, but they're also more integrated socioeconomically.  Racial 
integration is higher in inner-city private schools, and there are almost 
as many non-Catholic students as the faithful in inner-city Catholic 
schools.  Home schooling is growing, too, with hundreds of thousands if not 
millions of families nationwide taking the responsibility to teach their 
students rather than leaving them to the public schools.

Would you like to know how the very structure of public schools itself 
harms students?  Award-winning public schoolteacher John Taylor Gatto
criticizes public school: "...seven lessons are universally taught from
Harlem to Hollywood Hills.  They constitute a national curriculum...  These
are the things I teach, these are the things you pay me to teach."

Would you like it if public schools were conducive to learning?  "The first 
lesson I teach is confusion," says Gatto.  "Even in the best schools a close
examination of curriculum turns up a lack of coherence, full of internal
contradictions."  Lack of context, connection, and meaning are the norm.

Would you like it if the public schools taught students that all students are 
equal?  "The second lesson I teach is class position," he says.  "My job is to
to make them like being locked together with children who bear numbers like
their own...  If I do my job well, the kids can't even IMAGINE themselves
somewhere else, because I've shown them how to envy and fear the better classes
and to have contempt for the dumb classes.  Under this efficient discipline
the class mostly polices itself into good marching order.  That's the real
lesson of any rigged competition like school.  You come to know your place."

Would you like it if public school taught students to care about something?
"The third lesson I teach is indifference," he continues.  "I teach 
children not to care too much about anything... by demanding that they 
become totally involved in my lessons, jumping up and down in their seats 
with anticipation, competing vigorously for my favor...  But when the bell 
rings I insist they drop whatever it is we have been doing and proceed to 
the next work station.  They must turn on and off like a light switch.  
Nothing important is ever finished in... in any class I know of."

Would you like it if public schools taught students emotional independence? 
"The fourth lesson I teach is emotional dependency," he adds.  "As a 
schoolteacher, I intervene in many personal decisions, issuing a pass for 
those I deem legitimate, or initiating a disciplinary confrontation for 
behavior that threatens my control... Sometimes free will appears right in 
front of me in pockets of children angry, depressed, or happy about things 
outside my ken; rights in such matters cannot be recognized by 
schoolteachers, only privileges that can be withdrawn, hostages to good 
behavior."

Would you like it if public schools taught intellectual independence?  "The 
fifth lesson I teach is intellectual dependency," he goes on.  "Good 
students wait for a teacher to tell them what to do... The expert makes all 
the important choices; only I, the teacher, can determine what my kids must 
study, or rather, only the people who pay me can make those decisions, 
which I then enforce... Fortunately there are tested procedures to break 
the will of those who resist... We've built a way of life that depends on 
people doing what they are told to do because they don't know how to tell 
THEMSELVES what to do."

Would you like it if public schools taught students to have self-esteem, 
that they were capable and worthy of living?  "The sixth lesson I teach is 
provisional self esteem," he says.  "...I teach that a kid's self-respect
should depend on expert opinion...  The lesson of report cards, grades, and
tests is that children should not trust themselves or their parents but
instead rely on the evaluation of certified officials."

Would you like if it public schools respected student's privacy?  "The 
seventh lesson I teach is that one can't hide," he says.  "I teach 
students that they are always watched, that each is under constant 
surveillance by myself and my colleagues.  There are no private spaces for 
children, there is no private time...  Students are encouraged to tattle on 
each other or even to tattle on their own parents...  The meaning of
constant surveillance and denial of privacy is that no one can be trusted,
that privacy is not legitimate... children must be closely watched if you
want to keep a society under tight control.  Children will follow a private
drummer if you can't get them into a uniformed marching band."

Public schools were never structured to teach students that all men are 
created equal, that they are capable and worthy of living emotionally 
independent lives they care about, that they have a right to privacy that 
deserves to be recognized and enforced, or that they could figure out how 
the world works with their own mind.  In fact, public schools have never 
been predicated upon benefiting students.  "The basic justification for our
public schools (i.e., government schools) has always been 'for reasons of
State.'  The welfare of the child has placed a distant second at best,"
writes historian George Smith.

Public schools as we know them originated in the State of Prussia.  In 1809 
the Prussian army was defeated by Napoleon's.  In order to prevent this 
from happening again, compulsory, tax-supported factory schools were set up 
to produce obedient soldiers to be cannon-fodder, obedient workers for the 
factories and mines, officers to command the military, efficient clerks for 
the bureaucracies of the government and the industrial cartels, and an 
elite ruling intellectual class, the precursors of the Soviet nomenklatura, 
to make policy for the nation and manufacture consent to it, marginalizing 
anyone who dissented too far from the mainstream consensus.  The masses 
were to be taught to follow orders uncritically (as the Nazis argued that
they were only following orders in the Nuremburg trials), and the elite few
were to be raised to command the many.

Most of the United States of America already had schools.  They had charity 
schools for the poor, they had private "academies" where students could pay 
to be taught what they wanted to learn, and the closest thing to today's 
public schools were "common schools" supported by local taxpayers and
controlled by autonomous local school boards.

There was never any popular outcry for compulsory schooling.  Support for 
it originated amongst rich, religiously liberal Bostonians who wanted 
to use compulsory schooling to fight religious conservatives by forcing 
conservatives' children to be taught their liberal religious views.  They were
soon joined by followers of Robert Owen, one of the founding fathers of 
socialism, who wanted to use compulsory schooling to fight capitalism by 
forcing children to be taught not to care about themselves at all.  

Religious conservatives came to support compulsory schooling in order 
to fight the Catholicism of immigrants like the Irish and Italians, as well as
to "assimilate" their native cultures into the great American "Melting Pot."  
Then the "Red Scares" after the Revolutions of 1848 managed to put the fear 
of revolution among our own industrial poor into enough people to get the
first compulsory schooling law in the USA passed in Massachussetts in the
1850s, the first such law in that State since before the American Revolution
when the conservative Puritans ruled.  Even so, in the other States without
any tradition of compulsory schooling, it took until the 1880s to get
widespread passage of compulsory schooling laws.  Support for this may have
also been motivated by fear of literacy amongst formerly enslaved Americans,
which was increasing rapidly, and continued to do so until 1900, when it
leveled off.

Once the fundamental principle and practice of public schooling was 
established in this country, it was only a matter of time until the 
institution reached its logical conclusion.  "...after the turn of the 
century, during the 'progressive era,' there began a concerted attempt to 
destroy the system of local control through the 'unified schools' 
movement," writes historian George O'Brien.  "It took 50 years before most 
of the country was subjected to unified schools..."  Now curricula is 
centralized, as is hiring, through the requirement that teachers have
certificates of graduation from a State "teachers' college."  Instead of
being supported by students, parents, and local taxpayers, public schools are
now dependnt upon distant State governments.  Instead of teaching the
fundamentals of reading, writing and arithmetic that enable students to
learn independently, educational fads spread like the plague.  Instead of 
a well-rounded, diverse, informative curriculum, public school textbooks 
are a monoculture of watered-down superficiality.  Instead of accountability
through competition for the trust of students, parents, and taxpayers, public
schools that fail to teach their students never die, like some horror-movie
monster that stalks the earth undead and in search of young minds to feed on.

What's wrong with public school, and thus what's wrong with American
education, is its basic structure, one of centralized social control by
politicians and bureaucrats over the vulnerable minds of students.  It's
not that teachers are bad people who are too lazy to teach, or hate students.
Most teachers are good people who have to love students and teaching to work
at it as hard as they do.  It's not that taxpayers aren't willing to let
politicians spend more money on public school.  They're generosity has been
exhausted by the experience of having more and more of the money they
worked so hard to produce put into public school with the students having
less and less to show for it.  Instead of the elite few making all the
choices - where students will go to school, who will teach them, and what
they will be taught - we need to make structural changes so that students
and those that care about them most will make these choices for themselves.
They're in the best position to search for the best education, seek
advice from agencies that evaluate schools, and find the schools that best
fit the needs of students.

Would you like to live in a society in which the rights of students and
parents to choose the best school to attend, and in which the rights of
taxpayers to have less of their hard-earned money wasted?  If so, then
support the Parental Choice In Education Amendment on the California
Ballot this fall in any way you can.  Vote for it.  Get everyone you know
that cares about better education to vote for it.  Donate all the time
and money you can afford to Parents for Educational Choice, and get
everyone you know that cares about better education to do the same.  We
need all the help we can get in order to get better education for the
students we care about.

Tim Starr - Renaissance Now!

